    "text": "A publisher tests two Claude model families: a faster, lower-cost family and a more capable, slower family. The faster family accurately normalises short metadata records but misses dependencies in policy comparisons despite a complete brief; the slower family handles those comparisons adequately. The slower family adds substantial time to routine normalisation. Which allocation is best supported?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Choose one model family permanently using its reputation rather than the task-specific pilot results.",
      "B": "Move all work to the more capable family because its success on comparisons proves it is efficient for every task.",
      "C": "Keep all work on the faster family and request longer answers to compensate for missed dependencies.",
      "D": "Keep the faster family for validated normalisation and use the more capable family for demanding comparisons, retaining relevant checks."
    },
    "selectCount": 1,
    "correctAnswers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanation": "The pilot supports different Claude model families for different workloads. The faster family already meets the routine task’s requirements; the more capable family adds demonstrated value to difficult comparisons. Reputation, longer answers, or moving everything to the slower family does not follow from that evidence. Model family and thinking effort remain separate choices.",
